The payload of a 1-RTT packet laid out as quic-go lays it out, and its STREAM frames sized as
quic-go sizes them.

quic-go's appendPacketPayload (vendor/quic-go/packet_packer.go:1055-1092) writes the ACK, then the
padding, then every frame that is neither ACK nor STREAM — DATAGRAM, PING, the control frames of
the streams and of the connection, STREAM_DATA_BLOCKED — in an order drawn for each packet by a PCG
seeded from crypto/rand (:180), then the STREAM frames. The last STREAM frame of the packet goes
without its Length field, and every STREAM frame is sized as if it were the last: popStreamFrame is
asked with the length of that field added to the room left (vendor/quic-go/framer.go:442-446, :222-
229), and MaxDataLen reckons the field at one byte, two once the data is longer
(vendor/quic-go/internal/wire/stream_frame.go:136-155). No STREAM frame is taken with less than 128
bytes of the packet left (MinStreamFrameSize, vendor/quic-go/framer.go:177-184,
vendor/quic-go/internal/protocol/params.go:103).

Upstream ngtcp2 wrote the frames in an order of its own — the connection's control frames, the
streams', STREAM frames, the DATAGRAM after all of them, the padding at the end — gave every STREAM
frame its Length, and took a new STREAM frame only if 256 bytes of data fit or all of it did.

Now ngtcp2_ppe_encode_frame records where each frame went, and before a 1-RTT packet is sealed
conn_lc_quicgo_payload lays it out again: ACK, the others shuffled uniformly, STREAM frames in the
order they were taken with the last one's Length dropped; the padding is moved to after the ACK
(conn_write_pkt, and ngtcp2_conn_write_single_frame_pkt, which writes PMTUD probes: quic-go's probe
is its padding and then the PING). A new STREAM frame is sized by conn_lc_stream_max_datalen and
written without its Length when it would not fit with one — then it fills the packet and is last.
The order is inside the encryption, for the node alone to see; the Length is not only that: a packet
that ends in a STREAM frame short of full is one or two bytes shorter than ngtcp2 made it, as
quic-go's is, and packet sizes are what the path sees.

The DATAGRAM gets its room right after the ACK, before any control frame, as quic-go gives it
(vendor/quic-go/packet_packer.go:736-761); ngtcp2 took it after everything it had queued. One that
does not fit waits while the packet goes on with other frames (ngtcp2 answers WRITE_MORE, and the
client's loop goes on to its streams), is dropped when the packet has no ACK, and is dropped after
being passed over more than DatagramFrameMaxPeekTimes times in a row (:147), counted as quic-go
counts. With ack_datagram or lost_datagram set, which this client does not, the DATAGRAM keeps
ngtcp2's place.

Checked in test/ngpatch: the sizing against quic-go's MaxDataLen on 12 432 cases (tools/qgovec),
the layout and a chi-square over the 24 orders of four frames, the 128-byte floor at 128 and 127,
the DATAGRAM's room, and a PMTUD probe.

